Commands from any directory (use an absolute path to harness/build if needed):
python harness/build setup      # uv sync --locked, root and security tools
python harness/build context    # current static imports and public definitions
python harness/build maps       # Mermaid package structure/imports, Markdown index
python harness/build impact src/firewall_compliance/domain/source.py
python harness/build architecture # domain/analysis/parser boundaries
python harness/build quality    # Ruff formatting/lint and strict mypy
python harness/build test       # full tests with JUnit and deterministic Hypothesis
python harness/build smoke      # validated CLI JSON and existing Qt scenario
python harness/build package    # wheel and sdist, not a Windows executable
python harness/build security   # Bandit plus locked dependency vulnerability audit
python harness/build all        # all required checks, retains every exit code

Prerequisites: Python 3.12+, uv on PATH, network for locked packages and PyPI
advisories, Linux shared libraries required by Qt (including libEGL.so.1).
QT_QPA_PLATFORM defaults to offscreen. No cloud secrets are needed for synthetic
checks. Native Windows release gates: visible Qt smoke, DPAPI behavior and the
existing scripts/build_desktop.ps1. That script expects dev/.venv whereas uv
normally creates .venv: diagnose/configure separately before release packaging.

The runner writes unique ignored artifacts/harness/<timestamp>-<id>/ directories
with command logs, manifest, JUnit, security results and packages. It never
updates goldens or applies formatter fixes. Full stdout/stderr can contain
findings: only use synthetic data and review before sharing logs.
